    'human-rights-framework': {
      overview: 'Human Rights Law examines how rights are protected and how conflicts are balanced. Kellyn needs to understand the ECHR, the Human Rights Act 1998, qualified rights and proportionality, then apply those ideas to privacy, expression, protest, policing and surveillance.',
      sections: [
        { title: 'Human Rights Act 1998', body: 'The HRA gives Convention rights important domestic effect. Public authorities must generally act compatibly with Convention rights. Courts interpret legislation so far as possible compatibly with rights and may issue declarations of incompatibility where this is not possible.' },
        { title: 'Article 8', body: 'Article 8 protects private and family life, home and correspondence. It is a qualified right. Interference must have a legal basis, pursue a legitimate aim and be necessary and proportionate.' },
        { title: 'Article 10', body: 'Article 10 protects freedom of expression. Political and journalistic speech are strongly protected, but restrictions can be justified for aims such as public safety, prevention of crime and protection of reputation or others’ rights.' },
        { title: 'Article 11', body: 'Article 11 protects peaceful assembly and association. Protest cases often require balancing expression and assembly against safety, disruption and public order.' },
        { title: 'Proportionality', body: 'Proportionality asks whether the measure pursues a legitimate aim, is rationally connected to that aim, goes no further than necessary and strikes a fair balance between individual rights and the public interest.' }
      ],
      examples: ['Privacy and press freedom can create an Article 8 versus Article 10 conflict.', 'Police surveillance engages Article 8 even where the aim is crime prevention.', 'An unpopular protest can still be protected by Article 11 if it is peaceful.'],
      compare: ['Absolute rights cannot normally be balanced away; qualified rights can be restricted if the legal tests are satisfied.', 'A declaration of incompatibility does not strike down an Act of Parliament.', 'The HRA strengthens domestic remedies while preserving parliamentary sovereignty.'],
      mistakes: ['Do not say “human rights are absolute”. Many are qualified.', 'Do not jump straight to a conclusion without applying proportionality.', 'Do not treat Strasbourg decisions as if they automatically replace UK legislation.'],
      assessment: ['Apply Article 8 to a privacy or surveillance scenario.', 'Explain how Article 10 can be lawfully restricted.', 'Assess whether the Human Rights Act provides an effective balance between courts and Parliament.']
    },
